---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import StaticCode from "../../components/StaticCode.astro";
import { siteData } from "../../data/data";
import { site, targets } from "../../lib/site";

const toc = [
  { id: "what", label: "What h# is" },
  { id: "quickstart", label: "Quick start" },
  { id: "parts", label: "The four parts" },
  { id: "concepts", label: "Core concepts" },
  { id: "status", label: "Status" },
  { id: "layout", label: "Repository map" },
  { id: "next", label: "Documentation map" },
];

const quickstart = `git clone https://github.com/hana-games/hsharp.git
cd hsharp
npm install
npm run build
npm link

hs doctor
hs check examples/calculate.hs
hs build examples/calculate.hs --target rust --entry calculate --run --args 10,true`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Documentation"
  description="The precise guide to the h# language, profiles, CLI, code generation, and verified behavior."
  active="/docs"
  toc={toc}
  next={{ href: "/docs/getting-started", label: "Getting started" }}
>
  <h2 id="what">What h# is</h2>
  <p>
    h# is a small programming language built around a strict separation:
    <strong>meaning belongs to one semantic core; notation belongs to each user; output belongs to
    real target ecosystems.</strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    A program is parsed and checked once. The compiler produces a typed AST, runs a reference
    interpreter, and generates ordinary C#, Rust, Go, Java, C++, and JavaScript projects. The target toolchains then
    compile and execute those projects. Generated code does not call back into the h# compiler.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    Syntax may vary between users. Program meaning remains shared.
  </blockquote>

  <h2 id="quickstart">Quick start</h2>
  <p>
    From a clone to a checked and executed target. The command is called hs; hsharp and hcharp are
    aliases.
  </p>
  <StaticCode code={quickstart} language="bash" title="terminal" meta="clone, build, check, run" />
  <p>
    The doctor command records the detected toolchains. The check command parses and checks without
    generating output. The final command compiles the generated Rust project, runs the selected entry
    function, and prints one JSON outcome.
  </p>

  <h2 id="parts">The four parts</h2>
  <div class="grid grid-2">
    <div class="card">
      <div class="eyebrow">Semantic core</div>
      <h3 class="h3" style="margin-top:0.5rem;">Types, order, failures</h3>
      <p class="small" style="margin-top:0.4rem;">
        i32 and bool, immutable locals, checked arithmetic, short-circuit evaluation, and acyclic
        calls. The core is intentionally small enough to specify completely.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <div class="eyebrow">Profiles</div>
      <h3 class="h3" style="margin-top:0.5rem;">Seven keyword roles</h3>
      <p class="small" style="margin-top:0.4rem;">
        A profile is validated JSON. It can rename seven roles, not change the grammar or the
        semantics. Conversion is token-aware and preserves comments.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <div class="eyebrow">Code generators</div>
      <h3 class="h3" style="margin-top:0.5rem;">Six native projects</h3>
      <p class="small" style="margin-top:0.4rem;">
        {targets.map((target) => target.label).join(", ")}. Each backend emits a project its own
        toolchain can build without manual edits.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <div class="eyebrow">Verification</div>
      <h3 class="h3" style="margin-top:0.5rem;">Evidence, not optimism</h3>
      <p class="small" style="margin-top:0.4rem;">
        Expectations are written independently. {siteData.conformance.summary.targets} target
        executions currently pass with zero failed and zero blocked checks.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="concepts">Core concepts</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Canonical source</td><td>The shared source program written in standard notation.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Profile</td><td>Validated JSON that renames seven keyword roles for one person.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Typed AST</td><td>The normalized, checked program structure used by every backend.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Target</td><td>An ordinary C#, Rust, Go, Java, C++, or JavaScript project compiled by its own toolchain.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Manifest</td><td>Fingerprints that protect export and reimport from stale edits.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="status">Status</h2>
  <p>
    h# is a proof of concept, not a production language. Version {site.version} implements the
    documented core: functions, calls, immutable typed locals, conditions, returns, checked
    arithmetic, and profile-based keyword customization.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Current state</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Core</td><td>i32, bool, functions, locals, if/else, returns</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Checks</td><td>Types, arity, names, returns, call cycles, chained comparisons</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Profiles</td><td>Validated keyword roles; export/import with fingerprints</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Targets</td><td>C#, Rust, Go, Java, C++, and JavaScript generated, compiled, executed</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Editor</td><td>Visual Studio Code extension with live diagnostics and preview</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Not yet</td><td>Strings, lists, loops, recursion, modules, generics, async</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="layout">Repository map</h2>
  <p>
    The repository is deliberately small. Generated directories are disposable; profiles, examples,
    and the specification are inputs.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>Contents</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>src/</code></td><td>Lexer, parser, checker, interpreter, generators, CLI, and bridge.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>spec/</code></td><td>Normative specification and design decisions.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>profiles/</code></td><td>Standard and personal profile JSON documents.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>examples/</code></td><td>Programs used by the conformance corpus.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>tests/</code></td><td>Unit, profile, bridge, and conformance tests.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>reports/</code></td><td>Generated conformance, environment, and benchmark evidence.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>editors/vscode/</code></td><td>Visual Studio Code extension and its protocol client.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>site/</code></td><td>This documentation and showcase website.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="next">Documentation map</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Page</th><th>Start here for</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><a href="/docs/getting-started">Getting started</a></td><td>Install, first program, generated project, editor, and troubleshooting.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="/docs/language">Language core</a></td><td>The normative semantics, type rules, evaluation order, and error codes.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="/docs/grammar">Grammar</a></td><td>The accepted syntax, lexical rules, and parser restrictions.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="/docs/profiles">Profiles</a></td><td>Keyword customization, validation, manifests, and safe reimport.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="/docs/cli">CLI reference</a></td><td>Every command, flag, output convention, and exit code.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="/docs/targets">Targets</a></td><td>Generated projects, checked arithmetic, naming, and outcomes.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="/docs/verification">Verification</a></td><td>The evidence, reproduction commands, and explicit limits.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="/examples">Examples</a></td><td>Searchable programs from the real conformance corpus.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="/playground">Playground</a></td><td>Interactive demonstrations without executing h# in the browser.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="/vscode">VS Code</a></td><td>Highlighting, diagnostics, preview, install, commands, and limits.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</DocsLayout>
